# std::iter::from_fn

Kind: function
Defined in: `std::iter`
Signature: `pub fn from_fn<T, F>(f: F) -> FromFn<F> where F: FnMut() -> Option<T>`
Stable since: Rust 1.34.0

Creates an iterator with the provided closure
`F: FnMut() -> Option<T>` as its `next` method.

The iterator will yield the `T`s returned from the closure.

This allows creating a custom iterator with any behavior
without using the more verbose syntax of creating a dedicated type
and implementing the `Iterator` trait for it.

Note that the `FromFn` iterator doesn’t make assumptions about the behavior of the closure,
and therefore conservatively does not implement `FusedIterator`,
or override `Iterator::size_hint()` from its default `(0, None)`.

The closure can use captures and its environment to track state across iterations. Depending on
how the iterator is used, this may require specifying the `move` keyword on the closure.

# Examples

Let’s re-implement the counter iterator from module-level documentation:

```rust
let mut count = 0;
let counter = std::iter::from_fn(move || {
    // Increment our count. This is why we started at zero.
    count += 1;

    // Check to see if we've finished counting or not.
    if count < 6 {
        Some(count)
    } else {
        None
    }
});
assert_eq!(counter.collect::<Vec<_>>(), &[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]);
```

# std::iter::once

Kind: function
Defined in: `std::iter`
Signature: `pub fn once<T>(value: T) -> Once<T>`
Stable since: Rust 1.2.0

Creates an iterator that yields an element exactly once.

This is commonly used to adapt a single value into a `chain()` of other
kinds of iteration. Maybe you have an iterator that covers almost
everything, but you need an extra special case. Maybe you have a function
which works on iterators, but you only need to process one value.

# Examples

Basic usage:

```rust
use std::iter;

// one is the loneliest number
let mut one = iter::once(1);

assert_eq!(Some(1), one.next());

// just one, that's all we get
assert_eq!(None, one.next());
```

Chaining together with another iterator. Let's say that we want to iterate
over each file of the `.foo` directory, but also a configuration file,
`.foorc`:

```rust
use std::iter;
use std::fs;
use std::path::PathBuf;

let dirs = fs::read_dir(".foo")?;

// we need to convert from an iterator of DirEntry-s to an iterator of
// PathBufs, so we use map
let dirs = dirs.map(|file| file.unwrap().path());

// now, our iterator just for our config file
let config = iter::once(PathBuf::from(".foorc"));

// chain the two iterators together into one big iterator
let files = dirs.chain(config);

// this will give us all of the files in .foo as well as .foorc
for f in files {
    println!("{f:?}");
}
```

# std::iter::once_with

Kind: function
Defined in: `std::iter`
Signature: `pub fn once_with<A, F: FnOnce() -> A>(make: F) -> OnceWith<F>`
Stable since: Rust 1.43.0

Creates an iterator that lazily generates a value exactly once by invoking
the provided closure.

This is commonly used to adapt a single value coroutine into a `chain()` of
other kinds of iteration. Maybe you have an iterator that covers almost
everything, but you need an extra special case. Maybe you have a function
which works on iterators, but you only need to process one value.

Unlike `once()`, this function will lazily generate the value on request.

